## Established

- **Enrollment: 41% of households signed up for curbside composting in 2025.** The Gazette describes this as "city sign-up figures, 2025." The Council report gives it as "sign-ups as of Dec 2025." Both describe enrollment, not active use.
  - [Lumen Bay Gazette](https://lumenbay-gazette.example/2026/03/composting-milestone, 2026-03-10)
  - [Lumen Bay City Council - Annual Waste Report 2025](doc://council-waste-report-2025, 2026-01-15)
  - Independence caveat: the Gazette attributes its figure to city sign-up data, and the Council report was published two months earlier. The Gazette may be restating the Council figure rather than confirming it separately.

- **Landfill diversion: about 12,000 tonnes of food waste diverted from landfill.** The Gazette names the Harrow Point landfill and says "roughly 12,000 tonnes" for 2025. The Council report says "12,000 tonnes" without a time period. The 2025 date is inferred from the report's title.
  - [Lumen Bay Gazette](https://lumenbay-gazette.example/2026/03/composting-milestone, 2026-03-10)
  - [Lumen Bay City Council - Annual Waste Report 2025](doc://council-waste-report-2025, 2026-01-15)
  - Independence caveat: same as above. The Gazette figure is approximate and may come from the Council report.
  - Neither source gives a diversion rate (percentage).

## Contested

### Participation: 41% vs 34%

| Value | What it measures | source_name | source_url | publication_date |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 41% of households | Enrollment / sign-ups, 2025 | Lumen Bay Gazette | https://lumenbay-gazette.example/2026/03/composting-milestone | 2026-03-10 |
| 41% of households | Registered participation, sign-ups as of Dec 2025 | Lumen Bay City Council - Annual Waste Report 2025 | doc://council-waste-report-2025 | 2026-01-15 |
| 34% of households | Active use: bins used at least twice a month in 2025; door-to-door survey of 1,200 households | Coastal Sustainability Institute | https://coastal-sustainability.example/reports/lumen-bay-2025 | 2026-02-01 |

**Likely reason for the 7-point gap:** the two figures use different methods and measure different things.
- The 41% figures count administrative sign-ups.
- The 34% figure is a survey estimate of active use, with a threshold of at least twice a month.
- The Institute states that "sign-up counts overstate active use because many registered households never set a bin out" ([Coastal Sustainability Institute](https://coastal-sustainability.example/reports/lumen-bay-2025, 2026-02-01)).
- Both figures may be accurate for what they measure. No official set-out rate exists to check either one against.

### Cost per household: $18 vs $21 vs $24

| Value | Basis | source_name | source_url | publication_date |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| ~$18 per household per year | Blog estimate derived from the 2024 municipal budget; secondary, non-official | GreenLedger (blog) | https://greenledger-blog.example/posts/lumen-bay-compost-costs | 2025-11-05 |
| $21 per household per year | Official 2025 budgeted cost, "up from 2024 due to a new collection route" | Lumen Bay City Council - Annual Waste Report 2025 | doc://council-waste-report-2025 | 2026-01-15 |
| $24 per household per year (projected) | Forward projection after the hauler contract renewal adds a second weekly pickup from July 2026 | Memo: Organics Hauler Contract Renewal | doc://hauler-contract-memo | 2026-03-02 |

**Likely reason for the differences:** the three figures cover different years and are different kinds of figure.
- **Different years:** $18 reflects 2024, $21 reflects 2025, and $24 reflects service from July 2026.
- **Different types:**
  - $18 is an unofficial third-party estimate.
  - $21 is an official budgeted figure.
  - $24 is an official projection.
- **Consistent with a rising trend:** the Council report says 2025 cost more than 2024 because of a new route. That fits a 2024 figure below $21, but the Council does not give the 2024 number, so the $18 cannot be confirmed.
- **Scope and method:** none of the sources says whether its figure uses all households or enrolled households only as the denominator.

## Single-source

**Participation**
- 34% of households actively used compost bins at least twice a month in 2025, based on a door-to-door survey of 1,200 households ([Coastal Sustainability Institute](https://coastal-sustainability.example/reports/lumen-bay-2025, 2026-02-01)). This is also listed under Contested.

**Landfill diversion**
- The diverted food waste went to the Harrow Point landfill, which is the specific landfill named ([Lumen Bay Gazette](https://lumenbay-gazette.example/2026/03/composting-milestone, 2026-03-10)).

**Cost per household** (each figure also listed under Contested)
- About $18 per household per year, based on the 2024 municipal budget ([GreenLedger (blog)](https://greenledger-blog.example/posts/lumen-bay-compost-costs, 2025-11-05)).
- $21 per household per year in the 2025 budget, up from 2024 because of a new collection route ([Lumen Bay City Council - Annual Waste Report 2025](doc://council-waste-report-2025, 2026-01-15)).
- $24 per household per year projected, driven by a second weekly pickup from July 2026 under the renewed hauler contract ([Memo: Organics Hauler Contract Renewal](doc://hauler-contract-memo, 2026-03-02)).

**Resident satisfaction**
- 72% of composting participants rated the program "satisfied" or "very satisfied." Only participants were asked ([Resident Services Survey - Summary of Results](doc://resident-survey-summary, 2026-02-20)).
- The most common complaint was bin odour in summer, cited by 22% of respondents ([Resident Services Survey - Summary of Results](doc://resident-survey-summary, 2026-02-20)).

## Coverage gaps

**Agent status**
- **web_researcher: status "partial"** (error: null).
  - It ran 6 searches, and all returned the same 3 sources, so web coverage is narrow.
  - It found no resident satisfaction data: no survey results and no complaint data. The Institute survey reported usage only.
  - Its only cost figure comes from a secondary blog using 2024 data, with no official confirmation.
  - Its diversion figure comes from one news article and is approximate.
- **doc_analyst: status "ok"** (error: null).
  - It read all 3 internal documents.
  - It still stated that it "could not fully assess how well the program is performing" because of the gaps below.

**Source caveats**
- Satisfaction data comes from a single internal document. No public source covers satisfaction at all, so web_researcher's gap remains: there is no external corroboration.
- All doc:// sources are internal documents with no public URL.
- The Gazette may not be independent of the Council report for either the 41% or the 12,000-tonne figure.

**Missing by sub-topic**

1. **Participation**
   - No official set-out rate. The only active-use figure is the Institute's survey estimate.
   - No data for years other than 2025, so there is no trend.
   - No earlier-year enrollment for comparison.

2. **Landfill diversion**
   - No diversion rate, either as a share of total waste or of organics.
   - No total waste tonnage from which to calculate one.
   - No year-over-year tonnage trend.
   - The Council report gives no time period for its 12,000-tonne figure.

3. **Cost per household**
   - No official 2024 figure, so the $18 blog estimate cannot be verified.
   - No total program budget and no cost per tonne diverted.
   - The denominator (all households vs enrolled households) is unstated in all three sources.
   - Today is 2026-11-16, and the $24 figure was a projection for service from July 2026. No source reports actual 2026 costs.

4. **Resident satisfaction**
   - The survey's sample size, method and date are not given.
   - Non-participants were not surveyed, so the 72% does not represent all residents.
   - No formal complaint counts, such as service-request logs.
   - No trend against earlier surveys.
   - It is unclear whether the 22% odour figure is a share of participants or of all respondents.

**Overall limitation:** no source sets performance targets or benchmarks. The available data cannot show whether 41% or 34% participation, 12,000 tonnes diverted, $21–$24 per household or 72% satisfaction counts as good or poor performance.
